WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.890 --> 00:00:05.520
"Based on a True Story" is the oldest trick
in Hollywood's book, and horror fans have

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:08.250
seen variations on that idea countless times.

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:12.010
The blurrier the line between fact and fiction,
the bigger the potential scares, right?

00:00:12.010 --> 00:00:15.090
But sometimes, the stories behind the scenes
are even more terrifying.

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:19.400
Here are some horror movies based on even
scarier true stories.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:20.689
Dracula

00:00:20.689 --> 00:00:24.890
Bram Stoker's classic story has been revisited
countless times in cinema, making it some

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:27.050
of the most beloved source material of all
time.

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:31.769
But until Francis Ford Coppola's 1992 film,
Bram Stoker's Dracula, the connection between

00:00:31.769 --> 00:00:35.989
Dracula and the historical figure Vlad Drac
had never been depicted onscreen.

00:00:35.989 --> 00:00:40.280
If you think a pale, immortal vampire poised
to suck your blood is scary, consider "Vlad

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:45.019
the Impaler": the Transylvanian noble is thought
to have tortured and executed as many as 100,000

00:00:45.019 --> 00:00:47.520
peasants and civilians during his reign.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:51.550
Deeming the poor as "useless to society,"
he had them slowly impaled from bottom to

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:53.850
top, and then hoisted publicly for display.

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:56.789
So which is scarier: the fang or the pole?

00:00:56.789 --> 00:00:58.359
The Exorcist

00:00:58.359 --> 00:01:03.499
The Oscar-winning 1973 film The Exorcist was
based on the 1949 possession of a Maryland

00:01:03.499 --> 00:01:08.110
boy named "Roland Doe," who reportedly underwent
an exhausting battery of exorcisms performed

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:09.220
by Jesuit priests.

00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:13.320
While skeptics maintain that "Doe" was insane,
the phenomenon of "demonic possession" has

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:17.040
been traced to Biblical times and is taken
very seriously by religious experts.

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:21.280
While neither spinning heads nor projectile
vomit have been widely reported, levitations,

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:24.380
shaking beds, and speaking in tongues have.

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:26.130
Not scared yet?

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:30.469
Consider that the demand for exorcisms has
rapidly increased in recent years: in 2015,

00:01:30.469 --> 00:01:34.790
the Catholic Church reported having as many
as 100 requests at any given time.

00:01:34.790 --> 00:01:36.759
The Amityville Horror

00:01:36.759 --> 00:01:40.950
The house at 112 Ocean Avenue in Amityville,
Long Island, is an icon of the horror genre,

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:43.110
having inspired The Amityville Horror film
franchise.

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:48.240
But what transpired at the actual address
is more horrific than anything depicted in

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:49.240
the franchise.

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:52.850
There, 24 year-old Ron DeFeo brutally murdered
his mother, father and four younger siblings

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:54.469
with a shotgun in 1974.

00:01:54.469 --> 00:01:58.401
The hotly contested account in Jay Anson's
book details the bizarre events that followed:

00:01:58.401 --> 00:02:04.560
the house reportedly stood empty for 13 months,
until the Lutz family moved in—only to abruptly

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:09.209
flee after 28 days, claiming they had been
terrorized by paranormal phenomena.

00:02:09.209 --> 00:02:13.160
After extensive paranormal investigations
for decades, subsequent owners remodeled the

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:16.260
exterior, and it's no longer even listed at
112 Ocean Avenue.

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:23.610
The house is still standing, though—and
for $850,000, it can be yours!

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:25.580
The Serpent and the Rainbow

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:28.989
Zombies are all the rage, as The Walking Dead
and practically every other TV show and video

00:02:28.989 --> 00:02:30.060
game seems to prove.

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:34.090
In Wes Craven's 1988 voodoo horror film The
Serpent and the Rainbow, however, we find

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:36.030
a very different kind of zombie.

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:40.150
It's loosely adapted from a nonfiction book
of the same name by ethnobotanist Wade Davis,

00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:44.080
who outlined his expeditions to Haiti investigating
the bizarre case of Clairvius Narcisse, a

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:46.709
man who had reportedly "returned from the
dead."

00:02:46.709 --> 00:02:50.540
According to Narcisse, he was administered
a "pufferfish" toxin after offending a voodoo

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:51.540
sorcerer.

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:54.019
As a result, he collapsed and was pronounced
dead.

00:02:54.019 --> 00:02:58.140
But he was actually conscious during the delivery
of his death certificate, and witnessed his

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:00.500
own burial...from inside the casket.

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:03.982
He was recovered from his grave two days after
his funeral and was enslaved, forced to work

00:03:03.982 --> 00:03:05.849
on a sugar plantation.

00:03:05.849 --> 00:03:09.860
Narcisse was finally released and returned
to his village 18 years later, where the "dead

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:11.200
man" reunited with his sister.

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:12.200
So...happy ending?

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:13.200
Kinda?

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:16.110
The Terrifying Ed Gein

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:19.180
Some of the scariest and best-known characters
in horror have been based on the serial killer

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:20.239
Ed Gein.

00:03:20.239 --> 00:03:24.940
Gein's grisly murder spree inspired Leatherface
of The Texas Chainsaw Massacre, Norman Bates

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:28.569
of Psycho, and Buffalo Bill of The Silence
of the Lambs, to name just a few.

00:03:28.569 --> 00:03:32.530
In fact, Gein's actual crimes were so heinous
that he might be the most monstrous figure

00:03:32.530 --> 00:03:33.530
in American history.

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:37.230
The Gein family wasn't exactly a Norman Rockwell
portrait to begin with.

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:40.010
His father and two brothers passed away early
in his life.

00:03:40.010 --> 00:03:43.349
After the passing of his sole companion, his
beloved mother, Gein lived alone in their

00:03:43.349 --> 00:03:47.900
rural Wisconsin farmhouse, and the mild-mannered
handyman didn't attract much attention in

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:49.620
the 12 years following her death.

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:54.340
But when a neighboring hardware store owner
disappeared in 1957, Gein came under suspicion,

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:58.341
and a search of his property revealed the
decapitated woman in a shed, splayed out like

00:03:58.341 --> 00:03:59.760
a pre-skinned deer.

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:03.920
Inside the house, authorities found masks,
wastebaskets and lampshades made from human

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:05.959
skin, along with assorted human remains.

00:04:05.959 --> 00:04:09.879
In fact, some of what was discovered is far
too grisly to mention here, but suffice it

00:04:09.879 --> 00:04:12.180
to say, Gein's living room would make Leatherface
blush.

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:16.370
It was later determined that he'd also been
making late-night visits to nearby cemeteries,

00:04:16.370 --> 00:04:20.820
exhuming women who looked like his mother—whose
room was found boarded up and completely untouched.

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:25.220
Gein's story, even sans chainsaw, is truly
the stuff of nightmares.

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:26.220
Thanks for watching!

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:29.870
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:04:29.870 --> 00:04:33.190
And leave us a comment to let us know which
of these true stories is scarier than fiction...

